<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hovhannes Kajaznuni</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a>–<a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Hovhannes Katchaznouni</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hovhannes Kajaznuni was an Armenian architect and leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-revolutionary-federation">Armenian Revolutionary Federation</a> who served as the first prime minister of the First Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> from 1918 to 1919. In 1923 he published a critical report on his party's record. He later returned to Soviet Armenia and died in Stalin's purges.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hovhannes-kajaznuni.jpg" alt="Hovhannes Kajaznuni" width="320" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hovhannes_Katchaznouni_hat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born Hovhannes Ter-Hovhannisian on 14 February 1868 in Akhaltsikh, in the Tiflis Governorate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. After school in Tiflis he studied at the Citizens' Architectural Institute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, graduating with honours in 1893, and there joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-revolutionary-federation">Armenian Revolutionary Federation</a>. He worked as an architect in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batumi">Batum</a> and Tiflis; his best-known building is the Saint Thaddeus and Bartholomew Cathedral in Baku, completed in 1911. In 1911 he left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> to avoid testifying at a trial of party members, living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and Van until 1914.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>He sat for his party in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seym-river">Seym</a>, the Transcaucasian parliament, and joined the delegation that met the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at the Trebizond Peace Conference from 14 March 1918. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batumi">Batum</a> fell in April the Transcaucasian Democratic Federative Republic was proclaimed, with Kajaznuni among its ministers. At the Batum Peace Conference of May 1918 Ottoman forces invaded Erivan Governorate, and the federation broke up; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> declared independence on 28 May.</p><p>The Armenian National Council elected him prime minister on 6 June 1918, and he served until 7 August 1919, then went on missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. His son Aram was killed in June 1920, the second son he lost in battle. He became chairman of parliament on 4 November 1920, was arrested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> and freed in the February 1921 revolt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> he wrote Dashnaktsutyun Has Nothing More to Do, a report to the party's convention of April 1923 calling for support of Soviet Armenia, and left the party that August. In 1925 he returned to work as an architect in Leninakan and taught construction in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a>. Arrested in 1937 in Stalin's Great Terror, he died in prison. In 2006 Mehmet Perinçek, who denies the Armenian genocide, published translations of the report as evidence against it; Viken L. Attarian calls such texts falsified, and the report itself refers to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> or extermination of the Ottoman Armenians.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hovhannes Kajaznuni” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The republic was governed by the Armenian Revolutionary Federation, with prime ministers <b>Hovhannes Kajaznuni</b> and Alexander Khatisian and a parliament elected by universal suffrage, including women.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-republic-of-armenia">First Republic of Armenia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhaltsikhe">Akhaltsikhe</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/armenia">Armenia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.64,42.99&amp;year=1913" aria-label="Akhaltsikhe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hovhannes Kajaznuni"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5823 3965h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5823 3965h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.64,42.99&amp;year=1913">What was here in 1913? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
